(window.LADDER_CARDS["36·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["36·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"36·phys.1.1","q":"What does the counts-add technique say of two lamps, why must their light arrive independently, and when is “together” not a sum?","a":"The amount at each wavelength of the two together is the sum of their amounts, since no photon is from both lamps and counts of separate sets add; when one beam is split and rejoined, the waves keep time, and where they meet half a turn apart they cancel: then the waves add, not the amounts","w":["The amounts multiply, wavelength by wavelength; independence makes no difference; together is never a sum","The amounts add because the lamps have the same colour; with two lamps of different colours together is not a sum","The amounts add since no photon is from both lamps; together fails only when one lamp is far brighter than the other"],"why":"Two ordinary lamps never keep time for long: a fact by cases with a rate."},
 {"u":"36·phys.2.1","q":"What does a filter do to a spectrum at each wavelength, what does the through-filters technique say, and why is a pot of mixed paint not two filters in a row?","a":"It keeps a share t(λ) between 0 and 1, so S comes out as t·S; through two filters the light is t₁t₂S, in either order; in a pot some light bounces back off grains near the top and never passes through anything","w":["It adds its own spectrum to S; two filters give S + t₁ + t₂; a pot is two filters in a row","It keeps a share t(λ); two filters give (t₁ + t₂)S, and the order matters; a pot differs because paint is thicker","It keeps a share t(λ); two filters give t₁t₂S in either order; a pot is the same product, and 36h6 says so"],"why":"Multiplying, not adding: the pigment mix seen in the light itself."},
 {"u":"36·phys.3.1","q":"Why does a molecule remove only certain wavelengths, which wavelength does the gap-to-wavelength technique give, and how sure is it?","a":"It can take a photon only when the photon’s energy fits a gap ΔE between two of its steps; that is the light at λ = hc/ΔE; a gap known to a share s gives the wavelength to the same share s","w":["It takes every photon above a threshold energy; λ = hc · ΔE; the doubt in λ is twice that in the gap","It takes a photon only when its energy fits a gap; λ = hc/ΔE; the wavelength is exact whatever the doubt in the gap","It takes photons at random; λ = ΔE/hc; a gap known to 1% gives the wavelength to 10%"],"why":"A gap of 3.40 × 10⁻¹⁹ J takes light at 584 nm, known to about 6 nm if the gap is known to 1%."},
 {"u":"36·phys.4.1","q":"What are a stove’s warmth’s three numbers and its energy, what does the unseen-light technique say, and what does a prism show of two metamers?","a":"(0, 0, 0), with energy above 0; adding light from outside the window to any light keeps its colour and adds to its energy; a prism lands each wavelength at its own place, so two metamers make two different patterns: a spike at 580 nm one line, a red and a green light two","w":["(1, 0, 0), red, with energy 0; adding it changes the colour and not the energy; a prism shows metamers as one pattern","(0, 0, 0), with energy 0; adding it changes nothing; a prism cannot tell metamers apart","(0, 0, 0), with energy above 0; adding it keeps the colour and the energy; a prism turns one metamer into the other"],"why":"The two weightings of 36b4 pulled as far apart as they go."},
 {"u":"36·phys.5.1","q":"Why do the shares sent back, passed and taken add to 1 at each wavelength, what does the pane technique give, and why is that a little more than the straight path alone?","a":"Each photon does exactly one of the three, and counts add; a clear pane whose faces each send back r and which takes nothing passes (1 − r)/(1 + r); the straight path gives (1 − r)², and light bounced back and forward inside adds r², r⁴ and so on of that","w":["The shares are defined as thirds; the pane passes (1 − r)²; nothing is added by bounces","Each photon does exactly one of the three; the pane passes 1 − r; bounces inside take light away, so it is a little less than the straight path","Energy is lost at each face; the pane passes (1 − r)/(1 + r), less than the straight path because of the bounces"],"why":"For glass r = 0.04: 0.923 passed against 0.9216 straight."},
 {"u":"36·phys.6.1","q":"How can the sun peak near 502 nm and not look green, what does the glow-colour technique say of a lamp filament, and what does 2700 K on a lamp’s box promise?","a":"A colour is col of the whole spectrum, and a peak is one place on a spread; a filament near 2700 K peaks near 1073 nm, outside the window, since λmax = 2.898 × 10⁻³/T; the box promises three numbers near col of the 2700 K glow, a metamer, not that the lamp glows at that temperature","w":["The air removes the green; a filament peaks at 502 nm; the box promises the lamp runs at 2700 K","A colour is col of the whole spectrum; a filament near 2700 K peaks near 251 nm, in the ultraviolet; the box promises a spectrum equal to the glow’s","The eye has no curve at 502 nm; a filament has no peak; the box promises 2700 photons per second"],"why":"Doubling T halves λmax."},
 {"u":"36·phys.7.1","q":"Why does a thin gas glow in spikes where a hot solid glows in a spread, what does the lines technique say of a cool layer of the same gas, and what can then be read in sunlight?","a":"Each fall between two levels of the gas gives one photon that fits one gap, so the glow is a sum of spikes at λ = hc/(E_m − E_n); a cool layer in front of a glow takes away spikes at exactly those wavelengths, its gaps being the same; so the lines missing from sunlight tell which gases the sun’s outer layer holds","w":["A thin gas is cooler than a solid; a cool layer adds lines at new wavelengths; sunlight shows the sun’s temperature only","Each fall gives one photon that fits one gap; a cool layer of the same gas lets those wavelengths through and takes all others; sunlight shows no lines","A thin gas has one level; a cool layer shifts every line toward the red; the lines tell the sun’s distance"],"why":"Hydrogen: 656.4, 486.2 and 434.1 nm among its lines in the window."});
/* end phase F und_r36 */
/* phase F und_r37: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["37·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["37·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"37·phys.1.1","q":"Why is air stiffer under a sound’s quick squeeze than under a slow one, and what does the warm-air technique give for the speed of sound at 35 °C?","a":"The squeeze is too quick for its heat to leak away: the air warms a little and the warmth pushes back, so the stiffness is larger by the factor γ = 1.4; c = 331.3 √(308.15/273.15), about 352 m/s","w":["Quick squeezes make the air denser; about 310 m/s, since warm air is slower","The warmth of a quick squeeze pushes back, making the stiffness γ times larger; about 331 m/s, the same at every temperature","A slow squeeze lets air escape; about 366 m/s, 35 m/s above freezing"],"why":"About 0.6 m/s more for every degree."},
 {"u":"37·phys.2.1","q":"What does the moving-listener technique say, and what pitch does a cyclist hear riding at 10 m/s away from a still 500 Hz bell?","a":"A listener moving towards a still source at v hears it (c + v)/c times as often, and moving away (c − v)/c; 500 × 333/343, about 485 Hz","w":["Moving towards, c/(c − v) times as often, the same as a moving source; about 515 Hz","Moving away, (c − v)/c times as often; about 515 Hz","Moving away, (c − v)/c times as often; about 490 Hz, 10 Hz lower for 10 m/s"],"why":"Crests c/f apart arrive at relative speed c − v."},
 {"u":"37·phys.3.1","q":"What does the sound-cone technique say, why is the boom not one event, and what is the half-angle for a bullet at 686 m/s?","a":"A source faster than sound trails a cone of sound whose half-angle θ has sin θ = c/v; the cone is dragged along for as long as the source is faster than sound; for 686 m/s, sin θ = ½, so 30°","w":["sin θ = v/c; the boom happens once, at the moment the speed of sound is passed; 60°","sin θ = c/v; the boom is dragged along; 60°, since 686 is twice 343","tan θ = c/v; the boom is made once; about 27°"],"why":"The touching line is at right angles to the sphere’s radius ct, a distance vt from its centre."},
 {"u":"37·phys.4.1","q":"What does the bottle technique say, what plays the mass and what the spring, and what does a 119 Hz bottle hum when only a quarter of its volume is left as air?","a":"A bottle hums at f = (c/2π)√(A/(VL)); the plug of air in the neck is the mass and the air in the body the spring; a quarter of the volume doubles the note: about 238 Hz","w":["It hums at c/(4L), like a tube; the glass is the mass; about 476 Hz","It hums at (c/2π)√(A/(VL)); the neck’s air is the mass and the body’s the spring; about 60 Hz, since less air hums lower","It hums at (c/2π)√(A/(VL)); the drink is the mass; about 137 Hz"],"why":"f goes as 1/√V, whatever the bottle’s shape."},
 {"u":"37·phys.5.1","q":"“A jet makes its boom when it breaks the sound barrier.” What does the sound-cone technique say instead?","a":"The cone is trailed for as long as the jet flies faster than sound, and each place on the ground hears the crack as the cone sweeps over it","w":["The boom is made once, and its echo is what later listeners hear","The boom is made only when the jet slows back below the speed of sound","There is no boom unless sin θ = 1"],"why":"sin θ = c/v at every moment with v > c."});
/* end phase F und_r37 */
/* phase F und_r38: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38·phys.1.1","q":"What is spin, why can a swap of two electrons change their joint state only by +1 or −1, what does the swap-sign technique say, and why can two electrons still share an orbital?","a":"A vector in a space with two directions, up and down, carried beside the wave in space; electrons have no labels, so a swap can only multiply ψ by a factor, and two swaps change nothing, so the factor squared is 1; if the joint state changes sign under a swap, two electrons in the same one-electron state give the zero state; φ↑ and φ↓ are different one-electron states","w":["A turning of the electron about itself with any number of directions; a swap changes nothing at all; two electrons in one state double the state; they cannot share an orbital","A vector with two directions; the factor squared is 1; two electrons in the same state give the zero state; so no two electrons can share an orbital","A vector with two directions; the factor is always +1; two electrons in the same state repel; they share an orbital by taking turns"],"why":"The exclusion law is about which joint states exist."},
 {"u":"38·phys.2.1","q":"What does the shell-count technique say, which two facts give it, and why do the rows of the table not simply follow 2n²?","a":"Level n of hydrogen’s orbitals holds 2n² electrons; the orbitals of level n number 1 + 3 + … + (2n − 1) = n², and each takes two spins by the swap-sign technique; in larger atoms the inner electrons screen the nucleus more for some shapes than others, so the shapes of one level no longer share an energy and the order of filling shifts: rows of 2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32","w":["Level n holds n² electrons; one to each orbital; the rows follow n² exactly","Level n holds 2n² electrons; n² orbitals and two spins; the rows differ because heavy nuclei lose charge","Level n holds 2n electrons; n orbitals and two spins; the rows differ because spin is lost in large atoms"],"why":"Closed shells fall at 2, 10, 18, 36, 54 and 86."},
 {"u":"38·phys.3.1","q":"What does the upper-bound technique say, what in rung 31 does its proof use, and what two estimates of helium’s energy are made, with which one sure to lie above the truth?","a":"The average energy in any trial state is at least the lowest energy; the energy is a symmetric map, so by rung 31 it has perpendicular axes and the average is a weighted mean of the levels Eᵢ; first, the old state plus the average push, −108.8 + 34.0 = −74.8 eV; second, a trial charge Z′ = 27/16 giving −77.5 eV, a bound from above by the technique; measured −79.0 eV","w":["The average energy in a trial state is at most the lowest energy; so −77.5 eV lies below the truth","The average energy in any trial state is at least the lowest; the two estimates are −108.8 and −79.0 eV; the first is the bound","The average energy equals the lowest for the best trial state; so −77.5 eV is helium’s energy exactly"],"why":"A trial state can only overshoot."},
 {"u":"38·phys.4.1","q":"What does the two-shares technique say for each sign, and why do the two shares agree at high energy?","a":"A state of energy E holds on average 1/(e^((E − μ)/kT) + 1) particles of the sign −1, which sit 0 or 1 to a state, and 1/(e^((E − μ)/kT) − 1) of the sign +1, which sit in any number; at high energy the exponential is large, the ±1 beside it hardly counts, and both become e^(−(E − μ)/kT): states mostly empty do not show the sign","w":["Sign −1 has the − 1 below and sign +1 the + 1; they agree at low energy","Both signs give 1/(e^((E − μ)/kT) + 1); they agree everywhere","Sign −1 gives + 1 below, sign +1 gives − 1; they agree at high energy because every state is full there"],"why":"At low energy they part: electrons stack up one to a state, light piles in."},
 {"u":"38·phys.5.1","q":"What does the glow technique say, which three counts multiply to give it, and why does the total grow as T⁴?","a":"The energy per unit volume per unit frequency is 8πhν³/c³ times 1/(e^(hν/kT) − 1); the states per unit frequency, 8πν²/c³, times the energy hν of a lump, times the share 1/(e^(hν/kT) − 1) of sign +1 with μ = 0; put x = hν/kT in the total and it is T⁴ times a number free of T","w":["8πν²/c³ times kT; states times kT alone; the total grows as T","8πhν³/c³ times 1/(e^(hν/kT) + 1); states, energy and the share of sign −1; the total grows as T⁴ because light has four states","8πhν³/c³ times 1/(e^(hν/kT) − 1); states, energy and share; the total grows as T⁴ because each lump’s energy grows as T⁴"],"why":"ν³ dν gives four powers of T under x = hν/kT."},
 {"u":"38·phys.6.1","q":"Why is “two electrons cannot share an orbital” not the exclusion law, and why is −77.5 eV not helium’s energy?","a":"They can share an orbital with different spins; what they cannot share is one state; and −77.5 eV is a bound from above, with the measured value −79.0 eV below it","w":["They can never share an orbital; −77.5 eV is a bound from below","They can share with different spins; and −77.5 eV is the measured value, −79.0 the estimate","They can share one state when cold; −77.5 eV leaves out the spins"],"why":"The swap-sign and upper-bound techniques, read with care."},
 {"u":"38·phys.7.1","q":"What is an outer electron’s effective charge and how is it read from a measured energy, what does the screened-charge technique say, and why does the pull fall down a column?","a":"The charge it feels through the electrons nearer in, read as Z_eff = n√(I/13.6 eV); if each inner electron cancels a whole charge and each fellow of the same level only a share σ below 1, Z_eff rises by 1 − σ with each element along a row, and the pull with it; down a column a whole closed level screens the added charges almost entirely, so Z_eff creeps from 1.26 to 3.21 while n grows from 2 to 6: caesium 3.89 eV against lithium 5.39","w":["The whole charge Z; read as Z = I/13.6; it rises by 1 along a row; the pull rises down a column","The charge it feels, Z_eff = n√(I/13.6 eV); it rises by 1 − σ along a row; down a column Z_eff falls to zero","The charge it feels, Z_eff = √(I/13.6 eV)/n; it falls by σ along a row; down a column n shrinks"],"why":"I = 13.6 (Z_eff/n)²: up along a row, down a column."},
 {"u":"38·phys.8.1","q":"What does the shell-reading technique say, which premise sets the sizes of the peaks, and what do neon’s three peaks show?","a":"In the counts of electrons knocked out by light of one energy, each level and shape gives one peak at the energy that holds it; if each electron is as likely as any other to take the light, the sizes stand in the ratio of the numbers of electrons; neon’s peaks at 870.6, 48.5 and 21.6 eV with sizes 2 : 2 : 6 show level 1, then level 2 with ℓ = 0 and with ℓ = 1, and a gap of more than 800 eV between the levels","w":["Each electron gives its own peak; sizes follow the energies; neon shows ten peaks","Each level and shape gives one peak; the sizes follow the holding energy; neon’s peaks show sizes 2 : 8","Each level gives one peak; equal chances set the sizes; neon’s two peaks show 2 and 8 with no split by shape"],"why":"The shell-count technique read off a counter."},
 {"u":"38·phys.9.1","q":"What does the small-push technique say, where does its proof use that the technique is a symmetric map, and when is the shared-level technique needed?","a":"If the level E₀ of H₀ has a single shape u₀ of size 1, a small push λV moves it to E₀ + λ⟨u₀, Vu₀⟩ to first order: by the average of the push in the old shape; the proof moves H₀ across the product, ⟨u₀, H₀w⟩ = ⟨H₀u₀, w⟩ = E₀⟨u₀, w⟩, so the unknown w drops out; when a level has several shapes the push can mix them, and the level splits by the stretches of the grid of averages ⟨uᵢ, Vuⱼ⟩","w":["The level moves by λ times the largest value of V; symmetry is not used; the shared-level technique is for large pushes","The level moves by the average of the push in the old shape; symmetry is used to make ⟨u₀, u₀⟩ = 1; the shared-level technique is for levels with no shape","The level does not move to first order; symmetry cancels the push; several shapes each move by the same average"],"why":"Helium’s first estimate was this technique’s first term."},
 {"u":"38·phys.10.1","q":"Why does hydrogen’s lowest level not move, to first order, in an electric field while level 2 splits, what does the magnet-shift technique say, and how far apart are the three lines in a magnet of 2 T?","a":"In 1s the electron is as likely at z as at −z, so the average of eFz is 0; 2s and 2p₀ share a level and the field mixes them, a grid with stretches ±3eFr₀; in a magnet B a level with turning m moves by m μ_B B, so a line from ℓ = 1 to ℓ = 0, spins set aside, splits into three spaced μ_B B apart: at 2 T about 1.16 × 10⁻⁴ eV, 28 GHz, 0.040 nm for the red line at 656 nm","w":["The lowest level is too deep for a field to reach; level 2 splits in two; the magnet gives two lines 0.020 nm apart at 2 T","The average of z is 0 in 1s; level 2 splits by ±3eFr₀; the magnet gives three lines spaced 2μ_B B, 0.080 nm at 2 T","The average of z is 0 in every level, so none moves; the magnet gives three lines spaced μ_B B, 0.020 nm at 2 T"],"why":"0.020 nm at 1 T, and the spacing goes in step with B."},
 {"u":"38·phys.11.1","q":"Why are hydrogen’s corrections from speed of size α² times its energy, which two levels of n = 2 does the count split, and which pair does it leave together?","a":"The energy of motion is mc²(γ − 1), whose series gives a first correction ¾(v/c)² times ½mv², and (v/c)² = 2 × 13.6/511 000 = α²; the count depends on n and j only, so it splits j = ½ from j = 3/2, by about 4.5 × 10⁻⁵ eV or 10.9 GHz, and leaves 2s with the 2p of j = ½, which measurement finds 1058 MHz apart","w":["The corrections are of size α, since v/c = α; the count splits 2s from 2p and leaves the two 2p levels together","(v/c)² = α²; the count splits 2s from the 2p with j = ½ by 1058 MHz and leaves j = ½ and j = 3/2 together","The corrections come from the nucleus moving; the count splits by ℓ only; no pair is left together"],"why":"The 1058 MHz gap is from the electron’s push on the light around it."},
 {"u":"38·phys.12.1","q":"When may the slow-wave technique be used and what does it give inside a wall, and what does the loop-area technique give for the spring, and why is that a check?","a":"Where V changes little over one wavelength; there the wave is about (1/√p) e^(±(i/ħ)∫p dx), and inside a wall, where E is below V, it dies as e^(−∫κ dx) with κ = √(2m(V − E))/ħ; for the spring the loop is an ellipse of area 2πE/ω, so ∮p dx = (n + ½)h gives E = (n + ½)ħω, exactly the levels 35·phys.5 found another way","w":["Anywhere, turning points included; inside a wall the wave stops dead; the spring gets E = nħω","Where V changes little over a wavelength; inside a wall the wave grows; the spring gets E = (n + ½)ħω, which differs from 35·phys.5","Only for flat walls; inside a wall e^(−κL); the spring gets E = n²ħω, as in the box"],"why":"An anexact technique that lands on an exact answer."},
 {"u":"38·phys.13.1","q":"What does the leak-rate technique say, why does it need a band of states, and which earlier technique turns the total of sin²u/u² into π?","a":"A small steady push carries a system from a state i into a band of states, ρ of them per unit energy, at the rate (2π/ħ)|V_fi|²ρ; with one state alone the share swings up and back and never settles, and only a band wide enough to hold the narrowing peak turns the swing into a steady leak; 32e’s transform-energy technique, on a box of width 1","w":["The rate is (2π/ħ)|V_fi|ρ²; a band is needed so that energy is not kept; 22’s geometric sum gives π","The rate is (2π/ħ)|V_fi|²ρ; a band is not needed, one state fills steadily; 32e gives π","The rate is |V_fi|²/ħ; a band is needed to hold more electrons; 21b’s triangle gives π"],"why":"For 2p it gives a life of about 1.6 ns."},
 {"u":"38·phys.14.1","q":"What does the weak-bump technique say, what plays the part of the frequency in the transform, and why does the fading pull give the 1/sin⁴(θ/2) count only as μ goes to 0?","a":"A weak bump turns a wave of push ħk through θ with size f(θ) = −(m/2πħ²)∫V(r) e^(−iq·r) d³r, the bump’s transform; q, the change in the wave’s vector, of length 2k sin(θ/2), stands where the frequency stood; the fading pull g e^(−μr)/r gives f = −2mg/ħ²(μ² + q²), and only with μ = 0, the plain pull between two charges, is |f|² = g²/16E² sin⁴(θ/2)","w":["f is the bump’s height; θ plays the frequency; the count is 1/sin⁴ for every μ","f is the bump’s transform at q; k plays the frequency, of length 2k; the count is 1/sin²(θ/2) as μ goes to 0","f is the bump’s transform at q of length 2k sin(θ/2); the 1/sin⁴ count holds only for large μ, where the pull is short"],"why":"No ħ is left: the waves give the count that orbits alone would give."},
 {"u":"38·phys.15.1","q":"Why does “a field moves every level” fail for hydrogen’s lowest level, and why can the slow-wave technique not be used at a turning point?","a":"To first order the shift is the average of eFz in 1s, and that is 0, the orbital being the same in every direction; and at a turning point p = 0, so 1/√p blows up; the loop-area technique patches it with a quarter wave at each","w":["The lowest level is moved most of all; the slow-wave technique holds best at turning points","The average of z is 0 in 1s; and at a turning point the wave is zero, so nothing is needed","The lowest level has no charge; and at a turning point κ is not defined because V is flat"],"why":"38·phys.10 and 38·phys.12."});
/* end phase F und_r38 */
/* phase F und_r39: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["39·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["39·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"39·phys.1.1","q":"What does the level-spread technique say? Why does a row of N atoms give exactly N levels, and why is the band never wider than 4t?","a":"N atoms in a row, each leaking to its neighbours with energy t, turn one level E₀ into N levels E₀ − 2t cos(kπ/(N + 1)), k = 1, …, N; the N values of k give N different cosines, all the axes of a symmetric map on N places; and each cosine lies strictly between −1 and 1, so every level lies between E₀ − 2t and E₀ + 2t","w":["N atoms keep the one level E₀, filled N times; the band has no width","N atoms give 2N levels E₀ ± kt; the band grows wider without end as N grows","N atoms give N levels E₀ − 2t cos(kπ/(N + 1)); the band is 4t wide only for N = 2"],"why":"N = 6 gives E₀ ± 1.80t, ± 1.25t, ± 0.45t."},
 {"u":"39·phys.2.1","q":"What does the full-band technique say? Why does silicon take in visible light and let longer infrared through?","a":"A band whose every level is filled carries no current, since a push cannot move any electron to a level that is free; silicon’s gap of 1.12 eV is the energy of light of about 1107 nm, so every shorter wave, all visible light, can lift an electron across, and longer infrared has too little energy and passes through","w":["A full band carries the most current; silicon takes in infrared and lets visible light through","A band whose every level is filled carries no current; silicon takes in visible light because its gap is larger than any visible photon","A band carries current only when empty; silicon is clear to all light, since its lower band is full"],"why":"λ = 1240 eV nm / 1.12 eV."},
 {"u":"39·phys.3.1","q":"What does the gap-crossing technique say? Why do germanium switches leak more when warm than silicon ones?","a":"The share of the upper band’s lowest levels that warmth fills is about e^(−E_g/2kT), so a larger gap gives exponentially fewer carriers; germanium’s gap is 0.66 eV against 1.12 eV, and the exponent alone gives it about seven thousand times more carriers per level","w":["The share filled is about E_g/2kT, in proportion to the gap; germanium leaks more because its gap is larger","The share filled is about e^(−E_g/2kT); germanium leaks more because its atoms are heavier","Warmth fills half of the upper band in every material; germanium leaks no more than silicon"],"why":"For silicon at 300 K, e^(−21.7) ≈ 3.9 · 10⁻¹⁰."},
 {"u":"39·phys.4.1","q":"What does the donor technique say? Why does one phosphorus atom in five million matter more than all the warmth of the room?","a":"An extra electron bound by one charge inside a material that shields by ε and makes it move with mass share m* is held like hydrogen’s, with energy −13.6 eV × m*/ε², about −0.026 eV in silicon; that binding is about kT, so warmth frees almost every donor’s electron: 10¹⁶ per cm³, a million times the 10¹⁰ that warmth lifts across the gap","w":["The extra electron is held with hydrogen’s full 13.6 eV; phosphorus matters because it narrows the gap","The extra electron is held with energy −13.6 eV × ε²/m*; warmth cannot free it, so it carries current while bound","The extra electron is held with about 0.026 eV; one atom in five million gives 10¹⁰ per cm³, the same as warmth"],"why":"The binding is a few kT, against 21.7 kT for half the gap."},
 {"u":"39·phys.5.1","q":"What does the junction-voltage technique say? Why is the backward current Iₛ independent of the push?","a":"Where n-type with N_d donors meets p-type with N_a acceptors a barrier of (kT/q) ln(N_a N_d / nᵢ²) forms; a push V forward lowers it, and the current over it grows as e^(V/V_T); the push changes only the height that electrons from the n side must climb, and the flow back over the barrier does not depend on V: it is the −Iₛ of 39d1","w":["A barrier of (kT/q)(N_a + N_d) forms; the backward current grows as e^(V/V_T) too","A barrier forms that a forward push raises; the backward current is 0 at every push","A barrier of (kT/q) ln(N_a N_d / nᵢ²) forms; the backward current is fixed because the supply limits it"],"why":"With N_a = N_d = 10¹⁶ and nᵢ = 10¹⁰ per cm³ the barrier is about 0.71 V."},
 {"u":"39·phys.6.1","q":"Why does a full band carry no current? Why can the barrier not drive a current round a wire?","a":"A push cannot move any electron to a level that is free, since none is; and the barrier is balanced by the spill: joining the ends sets up equal and opposite steps at the contacts","w":["A full band has no electrons to move; and the barrier is too small, under 1 V","A full band carries current only backwards; and the barrier drives a current until the donors run out","The electrons of a full band are held by their atoms; and the wire has no gap for the barrier to push across"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r39 */
/* phase F und_r41: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41·phys.1.1","q":"What is a qubit, and what does the square-chance law say? Why does the state at 30° pass a 45° filter with chance cos² 15°?","a":"A two-way system whose state is a vector ψ = a|0⟩ + b|1⟩ of pairs of complex numbers, of size one, |a|² + |b|² = 1; measured along a right-angled pair d₀, d₁, it gives d₀ with chance |⟨ψ, d₀⟩|² and is afterwards in state d₀; the filter is 15° from the shaking, and |⟨ψ, d₀⟩|² = ((√3 + 1)/(2√2))² = (2 + √3)/4 ≈ 0.933","w":["A bit that is secretly 0 or 1; the law says it gives 0 with chance a; the 45° filter passes it with chance cos 45°","A two-way system with a + b = 1; the law says it gives d₀ with chance ⟨ψ, d₀⟩; the chance is cos² 15° because 45° − 30° is halved","A vector of size one; the law says the state is unchanged by measuring; the chance is ¾ at every filter"],"why":"No single hidden value of 0 or 1 says both ¾ along one pair and 93% along the other."},
 {"u":"41·phys.2.1","q":"What does the no-split technique say, and which fact about products does its proof use? What is E(α, β) for the state Φ, and what does each photon do alone?","a":"The state Φ = (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2 is not a state of the first qubit paired with a state of the second; matching ac, ad, bc, bd to Φ needs ac ≠ 0 and bd ≠ 0 with ad = 0, and a product is 0 only when a factor is (25); E(α, β) = cos 2(α − β); each photon alone passes half the time, whatever the angles","w":["Every state of two qubits is a pair of single states; it uses that products turn about; E(α, β) = cos(α + β); each photon alone always passes","Φ is a pair with a = d = 1; it uses that a product of sizes is a size; E(α, β) = cos²(α − β)/2; each photon alone passes with chance cos² α","Φ is not a pair; it uses that a sum is 0 only when both parts are; E(α, β) = 1 for all angles; each photon alone is stopped"],"why":"Such a state is entangled."},
 {"u":"41·phys.3.1","q":"What does the hidden-answers technique say, and why is S always +2 or −2 for one plan? Which angles make Φ reach 2√2?","a":"For every plan of answers the averages of products obey |E(a, b) + E(a, b′) + E(a′, b) − E(a′, b′)| ≤ 2; S = A(B + B′) + A′(B − B′), and B = B′ gives S = 2AB while B = −B′ gives S = 2A′B, each ±2; a = 0°, a′ = 45°, b = 22.5°, b′ = −22.5° give four terms of 0.707, 2√2 ≈ 2.83","w":["For every plan the sum is at most 4; S is ±2 because A and A′ are equal; the angles 0°, 90°, 45°, 135° give 2√2","For every plan the sum is exactly 2√2; S is ±2 because each product is ±½; any four angles reach it","For every plan the sum is at most 2 in size; S is ±2 because all four answers are +1; the angles 0°, 0°, 45°, 45° give 2√2"],"why":"Measured in 1982 and, with the gaps closed, in 2015: no plan of hidden answers fits."},
 {"u":"41·phys.4.1","q":"What is a density grid, and why do two mixtures with the same grid look the same? What does the half-pair technique say, and why can Seattle not signal to Victoria with it?","a":"ρ = Σ pᵢ ψᵢ ψᵢ*, for a system in state ψᵢ with chance pᵢ; the chance of reading d is ⟨d, ρd⟩, fixed by the grid, so the same grid gives the same chances for every measurement; whatever the Seattle lab measures on its photon of Φ, the Victoria photon alone has the grid ½I, so no choice in Seattle changes any chance in Victoria","w":["A list of the states ψᵢ; two mixtures look the same only when they are the same list; Victoria’s grid depends on Seattle’s angle, so a signal passes","ρ = Σ pᵢ ψᵢ; mixtures with the same grid differ along some filter; the half-pair technique says Victoria’s photon is always level","ρ = Σ pᵢ ψᵢ ψᵢ*; the same grid gives the same chances; Victoria’s grid is ½I only when Seattle does not measure"],"why":"The agreement shows up only when the two labs compare their lists, by an ordinary signal."},
 {"u":"41·phys.5.1","q":"What is a quantum gate, and how do H and the copy gate make Φ from |00⟩? What does the cold-qubit technique say, and why must a 5 GHz qubit be held near 0.02 K?","a":"A grid that keeps every product, run on purpose; H on the first qubit turns |00⟩ into (|00⟩ + |10⟩)/√2, and the copy gate turns |10⟩ into |11⟩, giving Φ; a qubit whose two states differ in energy by hf, left at temperature T, is found in the upper state with chance about e^(−hf/kT) when kT is far below hf; at 300 K, kT is about 1250 hf and the room shakes it at random, while at 0.02 K, hf/kT ≈ 12 and the chance is about six in a million","w":["Any grid of numbers; the copy gate alone makes Φ; a qubit is found upper with chance hf/kT; it is kept cold to save power","A grid that keeps every product; H on both qubits makes Φ; a cold qubit is always found upper; 0.02 K makes hf zero","A gate that forgets a bit; H then copy makes |11⟩; the upper state has chance e^(−kT/hf); a 5 GHz qubit works best at 300 K"],"why":"Some five thousand gates fit before the state is lost to its surroundings."},
 {"u":"41·phys.6.1","q":"What does the period-factor technique say, and why does N divide neither a^(r/2) − 1 nor a^(r/2) + 1? Which step of factoring does the quantum circuit do, and with which transform?","a":"If the period r of a is even and the remainder of a^(r/2) by N is not N − 1, then the gcds of a^(r/2) − 1 and of a^(r/2) + 1 with N are factors of N other than 1 and N; N does not divide the first since r is the least period, nor the second since the remainder of a^(r/2) is not N − 1; the quantum circuit finds the period, applying 32k’s fast transform to a register holding an even mix of every x","w":["If r is odd, a^r − 1 is a factor of N; N divides both factors; the quantum circuit does the gcd, by 4j","If r is even, r/2 is a factor of N; N divides neither because both are below N; the quantum circuit multiplies, by 27l’s elimination","If r is even and a^(r/2) leaves N − 1, the gcds are proper factors; N divides the second factor only; the circuit tries every factor at once"],"why":"For 7 by 15: r = 4, 7² leaves 4, and gcd(3, 15) = 3, gcd(5, 15) = 5."},
 {"u":"41·phys.7.1","q":"Why can the photons of Φ not have carried their answers all along? Why does a quantum computer not simply try every answer at once?","a":"That is a plan of answers, and the hidden-answers technique bounds every plan by 2, while Φ reaches 2.83; and a measurement gives one answer with its chance, so the transform must make the right ones likely","w":["They did carry their answers; the 2.83 comes from signals between the labs; and a quantum computer does read off every answer","A plan of answers reaches 2.83 too; and a quantum computer tries each answer in turn, very fast","The photons have no answers even when measured; and a measurement gives all answers with equal chance"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r41 */
/* phase F und_r42: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42·phys.1.1","q":"What is the energy of an arrangement of arrows, and what is its share at temperature T? What does the pair-agreement technique say, and why does it head to 1 as the temperature falls?","a":"E(s) = −J Σ sᵢsⱼ − h Σ sᵢ, the first sum over the links and the second over the dots; its share is e^(−E(s)/kT) divided by Z, the total over every arrangement; two linked arrows with no field agree on average by tanh(J/kT); as T falls, K = J/kT grows, the weights e^(−K) of the disagreeing pairs vanish beside e^K, and tanh K heads to 1","w":["E(s) = J Σ sᵢ; every arrangement has the same share; linked arrows agree by J/kT, which heads to 1 when hot","E(s) = −J Σ sᵢsⱼ; the share is E/Z; linked arrows agree on average by e^(−J/kT), which heads to 1 when cold","E(s) is the number of up arrows; the share is 1/Z; two linked arrows always agree"],"why":"At room temperature with J = 0.01 eV neighbours agree about 69% of the time."},
 {"u":"42·phys.2.1","q":"What does the ring-grid technique say? Why does the average arrow on a long ring head to 0 as the field is taken away, at any temperature above zero?","a":"Z for the ring of N arrows is λ₊ᴺ + λ₋ᴺ, where λ± are the stretches of the 2 × 2 grid T of link factors; for large N the average arrow is m = sinh b/√(sinh² b + e^(−4K)), and as h → 0 the top heads to 0 while e^(−4K) > 0 keeps the bottom away from 0","w":["Z is the sum of the entries of T; the average arrow stays at 1 when the field goes","Z = λ₊ᴺ + λ₋ᴺ; the average arrow heads to 0 only above a temperature of agreement","Z = 2ᴺ; the average arrow is tanh K, which does not depend on the field"],"why":"Walls are cheap on a ring: 2J each, against an entropy gain of about k ln(N²)."},
 {"u":"42·phys.3.1","q":"What does the average-field technique say? Why does m = tanh(τm) have a solution other than 0 only when τ > 1?","a":"If each arrow feels only the average of its q neighbours, then m = tanh((qJm + h)/kT); with no field a solution m ≠ 0 exists exactly when kT < qJ, and just below that m ≈ √3 (1 − T/T_c)^(1/2); tanh has slope 1 at 0 and bends down for x > 0, so the curve tanh(τm) starts above the line m, and meets it again, only when its slope τ at 0 is above 1","w":["m = tanh(qJ/kT) for every field; a solution other than 0 exists at every temperature","m = (qJm + h)/kT; a solution other than 0 exists only when τ < 1","m = tanh(τm) has only the solution 0, since tanh is below 1; agreement never begins"],"why":"For the square grid the guess gives kT_c = 4J; the exact answer of 1944 is about 2.269J."},
 {"u":"42·phys.4.1","q":"How is J for iron read from its measured temperature of agreement? What does the above-the-point technique say, and why does the lean grow without bound as T falls to T_c?","a":"The average-field technique read backwards: J ≈ kT_c/q = 1.381 · 10⁻²³ · 1043/8 ≈ 1.80 · 10⁻²¹ J ≈ 0.011 eV; above its temperature of agreement, in the average-field guess, a magnet’s lean in a small field is h/(k(T − T_c)); the bottom T − T_c heads to 0","w":["J = kT_c · q ≈ 0.7 eV; the lean is h/kT, bounded at T_c","J is read from the pull of two atoms’ magnets; the lean is h · (T − T_c), which heads to 0","J = kT_c ≈ 0.09 eV; the lean is fixed above T_c"],"why":"J is about 190 times the pull of a 1 tesla field on one atom’s magnet."},
 {"u":"42·phys.5.1","q":"What does the downhill-update technique say? Why is the stored pattern ξ a lowest point of the energy when wᵢⱼ = ξᵢξⱼ/N?","a":"Setting one arrow sᵢ to the sign of its field Σⱼ wᵢⱼ sⱼ never raises the energy; with wᵢⱼ = ξᵢξⱼ/N each term −wᵢⱼ sᵢsⱼ is least when sᵢsⱼ = ξᵢξⱼ, which s = ξ gives for every pair at once: E(ξ) = −(N − 1)/2, the least the energy can be, and −ξ is equally low","w":["Setting an arrow against its field lowers the energy; ξ is lowest because its arrows are all up","An update may raise the energy by |fᵢ|; ξ is a highest point, and −ξ the lowest","Updating never changes the energy; ξ is lowest only for N = 4"],"why":"From (+1, +1, +1, −1) one update brings back (+1, −1, +1, −1)."},
 {"u":"42·phys.6.1","q":"Why does agreement between neighbours not give agreement across a long ring? Why is kT_c = 4J not the true value for the square grid?","a":"Agreement between arrows k apart falls as (tanh K)ᵏ, so the ring has none in the whole; and 4J is the average-field guess: the exact value is about 2.269J","w":["Neighbours’ agreement adds up along the ring; and 4J is exact","A ring has no neighbours; and the true value is 8J","Agreement across a ring is tanh K; and 4J is measured on iron"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r42 */
/* phase F und_r43: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["43·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["43·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"43·phys.1.1","q":"Why may a long row of atoms be joined into a ring without changing its inside? What does the wrap-round technique say, and why are m and m + N the same wave?","a":"A row a billion atoms long has the same inside whether its ends are free or joined: joining only spares the ends, and the count of states comes out the same; on the clock of N the slide by one site has exactly N stretches, the numbers ω^m for the N hours m, and the vector it stretches by ω^m is cⱼ = ω^(mj); since ω^N = 1, m and m + N give the same amounts at every site","w":["Because atoms at the ends weigh nothing; the slide has one stretch, 1; m and m + N differ by one turn of the ring","Because a ring is shorter; the slide has N + 1 stretches; m and m + N are the same because N is even","Because joined ends hold more electrons; the slide has no stretches on a clock; m and m + N are different waves"],"why":"The sites live on one clock and the waves on another of the same size."},
 {"u":"43·phys.2.1","q":"Why does the statement H keep each wave of the slide? What does the hopping-band technique say, and how wide is the band?","a":"H is the same at every site, so HS = SH; if Sc = ω^m c then S(Hc) = ω^m(Hc), and the line of vectors S stretches by ω^m is one vector wide, so Hc is a multiple of c; an electron hopping by t on a ring of N sites has the N levels E₀ − 2t cos(2πm/N), one for each hour m, all in a band from E₀ − 2t to E₀ + 2t, of width 4t","w":["H keeps each wave because H is the slide; the levels are E₀ − tm; the band is 2t wide","H keeps no wave; the levels are all E₀; the band has no width","H keeps each wave because t is small; the levels are E₀ + 2t sin(2πm/N), in a band N wide"],"why":"Near the bottom the level is a parabola in k: a free electron with another mass."},
 {"u":"43·phys.3.1","q":"Why does each hour of a band take two electrons and no more? How does the two-per-hour technique give benzene’s six electrons a total of 6E₀ − 8t?","a":"Each hour is one state of the wave, and at most two electrons go in each one-electron state, one of each spin; the ring of six has the levels E₀ − 2t, E₀ − t twice, E₀ + t twice and E₀ + 2t, and the six electrons fill the lowest three hours, two each: 2(E₀ − 2t) + 4(E₀ − t) = 6E₀ − 8t","w":["Each hour takes two because the ring has two sides; the six electrons all sit at E₀ − 2t, giving 6E₀ − 12t","Each hour takes one electron; the six fill all six levels, which add to 6E₀","Each hour takes two because carbon has two bonds; the six fill the top three hours, giving 6E₀ + 8t"],"why":"Three separate double bonds hold 6E₀ − 6t: the ring is lower by 2t."},
 {"u":"43·phys.4.1","q":"Why do the filled waves of a block of copper make a ball of k? What does the ball-of-states technique give for copper’s top level, and why can only electrons near it change state?","a":"The hours on three edges make a grid of k, one per box of volume (2π/L)³, and the levels ħ²|k|²/2m rise with |k|, so filling from the lowest fills a ball; k_F³ = 3π²n gives k_F = 1.36 × 10¹⁰ per metre and E_F = 7.0 eV for copper; every state below is taken, and kT at room temperature is 0.025 eV, so only electrons near the top find a free state","w":["The waves make a ball because copper atoms are round; the top level is 0.025 eV; every electron can change state","The waves make a cube of k; the top level is 7.0 eV; only the lowest electrons can change state, having room below","The waves make a ball because of spin; the top level is 8.49 × 10²⁸ eV; electrons near the top are held by the nuclei"],"why":"At the top they move at 1.57 × 10⁶ m/s, even at the coldest."},
 {"u":"43·phys.5.1","q":"Why do the cells, and not the sites, become the hours when the sites alternate? What does the two-site-gap technique say, and where in the band does the gap open?","a":"The pattern now repeats every two sites, so the slide that looks the same everywhere is by one cell, on the clock of N/2; a ring whose sites alternate between E₀ + Δ and E₀ − Δ has two bands, E₀ ± √(Δ² + 4t² cos² ka), each of N/2 levels, with a gap of 2Δ between them; the gap opens at ka = π/2, where the cosine is 0, the middle of the old band","w":["Because there are fewer cells than sites; the ring has one band, moved up by Δ; the gap opens at the bottom","Because cells are larger; the ring has two bands that overlap by 2Δ; there is no gap","Because sites cannot be counted; the ring has N bands with gaps of t; the gap opens at ka = 0"],"why":"On a ring of 8 with t = 1, Δ = 0.5: nothing between −0.5 and 0.5."},
 {"u":"43·phys.6.1","q":"Why does a push not change the current of a full band? What does the full-clock technique give for the half full ring of six after one push?","a":"The current is the sum of sin(2πm/N) over the filled hours; a push moves every filled hour on by one, m ↦ m + 1, which is one-to-one on the clock, so a full clock stays full and its sum, the imaginary part of the roots of 1 added up, stays 0; half full, the hours −1, 0, 1 become 0, 1, 2 and the sum is 0 + 0.866 + 0.866 = 1.73: a current","w":["A push cannot move electrons in a full band at all; the half full ring keeps the sum 0 after a push","A push empties a full band; the half full ring gives 3 after one push","A full band carries the largest current; the half full ring gives −1.73"],"why":"Copper’s band is half full: a metal."},
 {"u":"43·phys.7.1","q":"Why does a perfect lattice not knock its electrons? What does the drift-current technique give for copper’s τ and its run between knocks?","a":"A wave of a perfect lattice is a shape of the statement, so its size never changes; what knocks is whatever spoils the repeat, atoms shaking with heat and stray atoms; σ = ne²τ/m with copper’s measured 5.96 × 10⁷ per ohm metre gives τ = 2.5 × 10⁻¹⁴ s, and a run v_Fτ = 39 nm, about 170 atoms","w":["Because the atoms are too small to hit; τ is one second and the run is one atom","Because electrons go round the atoms; τ = 2.5 × 10⁻¹⁴ s and the run is 7.4 × 10⁻⁵ m","A perfect lattice knocks at every atom; τ is the time to pass one atom, and the run is 0.25 nm"],"why":"The drift itself is 7.4 × 10⁻⁵ m/s at one ampere in 1 mm²."},
 {"u":"43·phys.8.1","q":"Why do the rates of independent knocks add? Why does cooling cut pure copper’s resistance 8 times and the nickel alloy’s only about twice?","a":"The chance of no knock in dt is (1 − dt/τ₁)(1 − dt/τ₂) = 1 − dt(1/τ₁ + 1/τ₂) + a dt² term lost as dt shrinks, so 1/τ = 1/τ₁ + 1/τ₂ and the resistivities add; the shaking part falls on cooling, from 1.68 to about 0.21 × 10⁻⁸ ohm metres, and the stray part, about 1.1 × 10⁻⁸ for one nickel in a hundred, does not: 2.8 warm against 1.3 cold","w":["Because the times τ₁ and τ₂ add; cooling stops the nickel atoms moving, so the alloy changes more","Because knocks of one kind stop the other; cooling changes neither metal","Because the rates multiply; the alloy has fewer electrons, so cooling matters less"],"why":"That ratio is how makers test copper for purity."},
 {"u":"43·phys.9.1","q":"Why does τ cancel in the heat-and-charge technique? How close does the technique come for copper, and why does diamond not obey it?","a":"κ = (π²/3) nk²Tτ/m and σT = (ne²τ/m)T both carry nτ/m, since the same electrons with the same run carry heat and charge, so κ/(σT) = (π²/3)(k/e)² ≈ 2.44 × 10⁻⁸; for copper at 293 K it gives 427 watts per metre per kelvin against 401 measured; diamond has no free electrons and carries its heat, about 2000, by shaking: the statement is a statement for electrons","w":["τ cancels because it is 0; the technique gives 401 exactly; diamond disobeys because it is too hard","τ cancels because heat needs no knocks; the technique gives 2000 for copper; diamond obeys it","τ does not cancel; copper comes out at 237; diamond disobeys because it is clear"],"why":"Good conductors of charge are good conductors of heat."},
 {"u":"43·phys.10.1","q":"Why is it wrong to say a ring of N atoms gives a band of N electrons? Why does a count of two electrons per atom not prove that a solid insulates?","a":"Two spins go to each hour, so the band holds 2N; and in three directions two bands can overlap: magnesium has two electrons per atom and is a metal","w":["It is right: N states hold N electrons; and two electrons per atom always fill a band and insulate","Because the ring has 2N atoms; and two electrons per atom make a solid clear","Because a band holds N/2; and insulating needs three electrons per atom"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r43 */
/* phase F und_r46: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["47·phys"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["47·phys"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"47·phys.1.1","q":"Which amount does each of the three symmetries keep: a shift in time, a slide, a turn of the phase? What does the phase-keeps technique say, and why do the V terms cancel?","a":"A shift in time keeps energy, a slide in place momentum, a turn of the phase charge; if ψ obeys the wave law and dies away at both ends, the total of |ψ|² does not change in time, because its rate is a flow out of the ends; the Vψ terms cancel because V is real, the same in the wave law and its flip","w":["Momentum, energy, mass; the total of ψ is 0; the V terms cancel because V is 0","Energy, charge, momentum; |ψ|² is the same at every point; the V terms cancel because V is small","Energy, momentum, spin; the total of |ψ|² grows with time; the V terms do not cancel"],"why":"The chance flows at the particle’s speed."},
 {"u":"47·phys.2.1","q":"What is isospin? How does the difference 0.764 MeV between tritium and helium-3 show that the nuclear pull treats protons and neutrons alike?","a":"The proton and neutron treated as the up and down vectors of a two-direction space, with the nuclear pull unchanged by every length-keeping grid of ℂ² of determinant 1; helium-3 is less bound by 0.764 MeV, and the electric push of its two protons at about 1.9 fm is 1.44/1.9 ≈ 0.76 MeV, so the whole difference is electric and the nuclear pull is the same","w":["The spin of a nucleus; the difference shows the nuclear pull is stronger on protons","The charge of a nucleus; 0.764 MeV is the mass of an electron","The count of neutrons; the difference shows that neutrons are not pulled at all"],"why":"A symmetry of the nuclear pull, broken by charge."},
 {"u":"47·phys.3.1","q":"What is strangeness, and why widen SU(2) to SU(3)? How was the mass of the Ω⁻ predicted, and how close was it?","a":"A kept amount found in the 1950s, kept by the nuclear pull and not by the weak one; SU(3) adds a third, strange direction, and its pieces of 8 and 10 fit the light heavy particles exactly; the piece of 10 has rows of 4, 3, 2 at 1232, 1384.6 and 1531.8 MeV, nearly even steps of about 150, so the row of 1 was put near 1682 MeV, and it was found in 1964 at 1672.45 MeV, within 0.6%","w":["The charge of a quark; SU(3) is used because there are three pions; the Ω⁻ was found first and fitted afterwards","A kind of spin; SU(3) because space has three directions; the mass was predicted exactly","The mass of a particle; SU(3) because 3 is prime; the prediction was wrong by half"],"why":"The prediction was from the representation alone."},
 {"u":"47·phys.4.1","q":"What are the charges of the up, down and strange quarks, and how do they give the charges of p and n? Why does the Δ⁺⁺ need each quark to carry a third label?","a":"+2/3, −1/3 and −1/3; p = uud has 2/3 + 2/3 − 1/3 = 1 and n = udd has 2/3 − 1/3 − 1/3 = 0; the Δ⁺⁺ is three up quarks with spins all up in the same orbital, three identical particles in one state, which the swap sign makes zero unless they can differ in a third label with three values","w":["+1, 0 and −1; p = u and n = d; the Δ⁺⁺ needs the label to have charge +2","+1/3, −2/3, −2/3; p = udd; the label is needed to tell quarks from antiquarks","+2/3, −1/3, −1/3; p = udd and n = uud; the label is needed because the Δ⁺⁺ has no spin"],"why":"The label is called colour; only the name is borrowed."},
 {"u":"47·phys.5.1","q":"What is the binding energy of a nucleus? What do the four terms of the mass technique stand for, and why does splitting uranium release energy?","a":"The missing mass times c²: the energy needed to pull the nucleus apart; the terms are the bulk, less the surface, less the push of the Z protons, less the cost of unequal numbers of protons and neutrons; uranium-238 is bound by about 7.6 MeV each and nuclei of half its size by about 8.5, so splitting releases about 0.9 × 238 ≈ 200 MeV","w":["The mass of the nucleus; the four terms are the four forces; splitting releases energy because uranium is heavy","The energy of its electrons; the terms are four kinds of quark; splitting uranium takes energy in","The charge times the size; the terms stand for proton, neutron, electron and neutrino; energy comes from the electrons"],"why":"Iron-56 comes out at 8.65 MeV each, measured 8.79."},
 {"u":"47·phys.6.1","q":"Which amounts must every decay keep, and which of them come from a symmetry of footing 1? Why is p → e⁺ + π⁰ forbidden though it keeps charge and energy?","a":"Energy, momentum and charge, which come from the three symmetries of footing 1, and also the heavy count and the light count, laws with no known exception; p → e⁺ + π⁰ takes the heavy count from 1 to 0","w":["Only energy; all come from symmetries; the decay is forbidden because the proton is too light","Mass and spin; none comes from a symmetry; the decay is forbidden because charge changes","Energy, momentum, charge and strangeness, all from symmetries; the decay is allowed and seen"],"why":"Not seen: the proton lives longer than 10³⁴ years."},
 {"u":"47·phys.7.1","q":"Why are the proton and neutron masses not equal if isospin is a symmetry? Why does a nucleus weigh less than its parts?","a":"Isospin is broken by the electric push and the quark masses, leaving them 1.293 MeV apart; and a nucleus weighs less by B/c², the binding energy: 2.224 MeV for the deuteron","w":["They are equal; and a nucleus weighs the sum of its parts","Because the neutron has charge; and a nucleus weighs more than its parts","Because isospin is exact; and the missing mass is the electrons"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r46 */
